{"prdMarkdown":"# Product Requirements Document: Password Reset via Email\n\n## Problem\nCurrently, users of the Ledger API service have no self-service mechanism to recover their accounts if they forget their passwords. Without an automated reset flow, users risk permanent lockout, leading to poor user experience and potential manual support overhead.\n\n## Goals\n- Provide a secure, self-service password reset flow initiated via user email.\n- Send a password reset link to the user's registered email address that expires exactly 1 hour after issuance.\n- Allow users to enter and confirm a new password upon following the valid reset link.\n- Protect user accounts by ensuring reset tokens cannot be reused and that requests do not expose user existence (enumeration defense).\n\n## Non-goals\n- Single Sign-On (SSO) integration or OAuth-based recovery (confirmed that all current users authenticate via standard credentials).\n- Multi-factor authentication (MFA) or SMS-based password recovery.\n- Updating other user profile or account settings as part of this flow.\n\n## User Impact\n- **Target Audience:** All registered users in `models.py`.\n- **User Experience:** Users can click a \"Forgot Password\" link, submit their email address, and receive a secure link within minutes. Upon clicking the link within 1 hour, they can set a new password and regain access to the application.\n\n## Open Product Questions\n- Should the password reset request endpoint return a generic confirmation message (e.g., \"If your email is registered, you will receive a reset link\") regardless of whether the submitted email exists, to prevent account enumeration?\n- What sender email address, display name, subject line, and email template copy should be used for delivering the reset link to users?","trdMarkdown":"# Technical Requirements Document: Password Reset via Email\n\n## Architecture Notes\n- **Service Context:** The application is a Flask 3.0 service running with `Flask-SQLAlchemy` and `gunicorn` as defined in `requirements.txt` and `app.py`.\n- **Endpoints (`app.py`):**\n  - `POST /auth/forgot-password` (or equivalent): Accepts an email, locates the user, generates a token, and triggers an email.\n  - `GET /auth/reset-password`: Validates the token and renders the reset form.\n  - `POST /auth/reset-password`: Validates the token, updates the password hash, and invalidates the token.\n- **Data Model (`models.py`):**\n  - Update `User` model to accommodate reset token handling (e.g., `reset_token_hash` and `reset_token_expiration` columns).\n- **Templates & Static Assets:**\n  - Render password reset forms reusing `templates/index.html` conventions and styling from `static/style.css`.\n- **Email Delivery:**\n  - `app.py` currently lacks any mailer configuration. An email client library and configuration (SMTP or transactional API) must be introduced.\n\n## Edge Cases & Dependencies\n- **Mailer Dependency (`requirements.txt`):** No email client library (such as `Flask-Mail`, SendGrid, or Resend) is currently listed in `requirements.txt`.\n- **Schema Migrations (`requirements.txt`):** No migration framework (such as `Flask-Migrate` / Alembic) exists to apply new columns on `models.py` in existing environments.\n- **Token Expiry (1 hour):** Reset tokens must strictly expire after 60 minutes.\n- **Token Invalidation on Use:** Reset tokens must be cleared or marked used immediately after a successful reset to prevent reuse attacks within the 1-hour window.\n- **Multiple Reset Requests:** Triggering a new reset request must invalidate any prior pending reset tokens for that user.\n- **Account Enumeration:** The request handler in `app.py` should respond identically whether an email is found or not.\n\n## Engineering Questions\n- Which email delivery service or provider (e.g., SMTP, SendGrid, AWS SES, Resend) should be adopted and configured in `app.py`?\n- Should reset tokens be stored as hashed tokens in `models.py` with an expiration timestamp, or should we use stateless cryptographic tokens (e.g., `itsdangerous`)?\n- Should `Flask-Migrate` / Alembic be introduced to `requirements.txt` to manage database schema updates for `models.py`?\n\n## Estimate\n- **Effort:** 2-4 days (up to 9 days worst case depending on email provider procurement and database migration strategy)."}